{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01564PXijzHcFYbMoyt8aASX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd023-fold\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/fold: Wrap Long Lines to a Specified Width\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  macOS BSD fold differs from GNU fold in multi-byte character handling and some edge cases\n6\t  with the -s flag. Scripts that wrap long lines for terminal display or fixed-width output\n7\t  rely on GNU fold. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n8\t  (gfold, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations and input types,\n9\t  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Wrap lines in each input file to a maximum width, inserting newlines at the specified column boundary, matching GNU fold behavior.\n13\t  - G2: Support -w to set the maximum line width (default 80 columns).\n14\t  - G3: Support -b to count bytes instead of columns when determining wrap points.\n15\t  - G4: Support -s to break lines at word boundaries (last space at or before the wrap column) rather than hard column positions.\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Core line wrapping\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: Must read each file (or stdin when no arguments are given) and write each line wrapped to at most W characters (columns by default, bytes with -b). The default width is 80.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: Lines shorter than or equal to the maximum width must be written unchanged (including the trailing newline).\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: Lines longer than the maximum width must be split by inserting a newline after exactly W columns (or bytes with -b). Wrapping is applied repeatedly until the remaining portion fits within the width.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: The final segment of a wrapped line retains the original newline if the source line ended with one; a line without a trailing newline produces a final segment without one.\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Width and byte-mode flags (-w, -b)\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: -w N must set the maximum line width to N. N must be a positive integer. If N is 0 or negative, fold must exit with an error message to stderr and exit code 1.\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: By default, width is measured in display columns. Tab characters count as the number of columns needed to reach the next tab stop (tab stops every 8 columns). Other control characters count as one column.\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: -b must measure width in bytes rather than columns. Each byte counts as 1 regardless of whether it is a tab or multi-byte character. This disables tab-stop expansion for width measurement.\n39\t\n40\t  R3:\n41\t    title: Space-break mode (-s)\n42\t    items:\n43\t      - R3.1:\n44\t          text: -s must break lines at the last space character at or before the wrap column, rather than at an exact column position.\n45\t      - R3.2:\n46\t          text: If no space character appears within the first W columns of a segment, -s must fall back to breaking at exactly W columns (same behavior as without -s).\n47\t      - R3.3:\n48\t          text: The space character at the break point is written as the last character of the current output line before the inserted newline. The next line starts with the character immediately following the space.\n49\t      - R3.4:\n50\t          text: -s is compatible with -b; space detection uses byte positions when -b is active.\n51\t\n52\t  R4:\n53\t    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n54\t    items:\n55\t      - R4.1:\n56\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n57\t      - R4.2:\n58\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n59\t      - R4.3:\n60\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n61\t      - R4.4:\n62\t          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n63\t\n64\tnon_goals:\n65\t  - cmd/fold does not implement Unicode grapheme cluster width calculation; it treats each non-tab byte as one column, matching the GNU reference binary behavior under LC_ALL=C.\n66\t  - cmd/fold does not implement --help or --version beyond the standard behavior.\n67\t\n68\tacceptance_criteria:\n69\t  - id: AC1\n70\t    criterion: \"printf 'abcdefghij' | fold -w 4 produces 'abcd\\\\nefgh\\\\nij' with no trailing newline, matching gfold -w 4.\"\n71\t    traces:\n72\t      - R2.1\n73\t  - id: AC2\n74\t    criterion: \"printf 'hello world foo bar' | fold -w 11 -s produces 'hello world\\\\nfoo bar' without splitting words, matching gfold -w 11 -s.\"\n75\t    traces:\n76\t      - R2.1\n77\t      - R3.1\n78\t      - R3.2\n79\t      - R3.4\n80\t  - id: AC3\n81\t    criterion: \"A line of exactly 80 characters passes through unchanged, matching gfold default.\"\n82\t    traces:\n83\t      - R1.1\n84\t      - R1.2\n85\t      - R1.3\n86\t      - R1.4\n87\t  - id: AC4\n88\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\tb' | fold -w 9 wraps at the correct column accounting for tab stops, matching gfold -w 9.\"\n89\t    traces:\n90\t      - R2.1\n91\t  - id: AC5\n92\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gfold passes for all required test cases.\"\n93\t    traces:\n94\t      - R1.1\n95\t      - R1.2\n96\t      - R1.3\n97\t      - R1.4\n98\t  - id: AC6\n99\t    criterion: \"cmd/fold compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n100\t    traces:\n101\t      - R1.1\n102\t      - R1.2\n103\t      - R1.3\n104\t      - R1.4\n105\t      - R2.1\n106\t      - R2.2\n107\t      - R2.3\n108\t      - R3.1\n109\t      - R3.2\n110\t      - R3.3\n111\t      - R3.4\n112\t      - R4.1\n113\t      - R4.2\n114\t      - R4.3\n115\t      - R4.4\n116\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f3326ee4-d90b-4fb3-a51e-e09ee1b69d8f","uuid":"df8b71db-c379-4d39-9ed3-03bd21c9c88f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T14:01:08.277Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5266/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd023-fold.yaml","content":"id: srd023-fold\ntitle: \"cmd/fold: Wrap Long Lines to a Specified Width\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD fold differs from GNU fold in multi-byte character handling and some edge cases\n  with the -s flag. Scripts that wrap long lines for terminal display or fixed-width output\n  rely on GNU fold. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n  (gfold, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations and input types,\n  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Wrap lines in each input file to a maximum width, inserting newlines at the specified column boundary, matching GNU fold behavior.\n  - G2: Support -w to set the maximum line width (default 80 columns).\n  - G3: Support -b to count bytes instead of columns when determining wrap points.\n  - G4: Support -s to break lines at word boundaries (last space at or before the wrap column) rather than hard column positions.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core line wrapping\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must read each file (or stdin when no arguments are given) and write each line wrapped to at most W characters (columns by default, bytes with -b). The default width is 80.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Lines shorter than or equal to the maximum width must be written unchanged (including the trailing newline).\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Lines longer than the maximum width must be split by inserting a newline after exactly W columns (or bytes with -b). Wrapping is applied repeatedly until the remaining portion fits within the width.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: The final segment of a wrapped line retains the original newline if the source line ended with one; a line without a trailing newline produces a final segment without one.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Width and byte-mode flags (-w, -b)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: -w N must set the maximum line width to N. N must be a positive integer. If N is 0 or negative, fold must exit with an error message to stderr and exit code 1.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: By default, width is measured in display columns. Tab characters count as the number of columns needed to reach the next tab stop (tab stops every 8 columns). Other control characters count as one column.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: -b must measure width in bytes rather than columns. Each byte counts as 1 regardless of whether it is a tab or multi-byte character. This disables tab-stop expansion for width measurement.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Space-break mode (-s)\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: -s must break lines at the last space character at or before the wrap column, rather than at an exact column position.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: If no space character appears within the first W columns of a segment, -s must fall back to breaking at exactly W columns (same behavior as without -s).\n      - R3.3:\n          text: The space character at the break point is written as the last character of the current output line before the inserted newline. The next line starts with the character immediately following the space.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -s is compatible with -b; space detection uses byte positions when -b is active.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4:\n          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/fold does not implement Unicode grapheme cluster width calculation; it treats each non-tab byte as one column, matching the GNU reference binary behavior under LC_ALL=C.\n  - cmd/fold does not implement --help or --version beyond the standard behavior.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"printf 'abcdefghij' | fold -w 4 produces 'abcd\\\\nefgh\\\\nij' with no trailing newline, matching gfold -w 4.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"printf 'hello world foo bar' | fold -w 11 -s produces 'hello world\\\\nfoo bar' without splitting words, matching gfold -w 11 -s.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"A line of exactly 80 characters passes through unchanged, matching gfold default.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\tb' | fold -w 9 wraps at the correct column accounting for tab stops, matching gfold -w 9.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gfold passes for all required test cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/fold compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}}}
